WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.733
Generators.

2
00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:08.000
They let you produce values one at a time, on demand, so even huge data fits in tiny memory.

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.133
Put yield in a function and it becomes a generator.

4
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:13.967
Calling it runs nothing yet.

5
00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:18.800
Each request for the next value runs the body up to a yield, then freezes.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.100
Here's the cycle.

7
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.400
The consumer asks for the next value.

8
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.667
The body runs until it hits yield.

9
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:30.667
It freezes there with all its local variables intact, and waits to be asked again.

10
00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:33.133
Compare it with a list.

11
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:35.867
A list loads everything first, then you work.

12
00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:40.433
A generator pulls one item through at a time, so memory stays constant.

13
00:00:41.633 --> 00:00:43.300
Let's stream a spacecraft log.

14
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.933
We read line by line and yield only the thruster lines.

15
00:00:47.233 --> 00:00:49.498
Creating the generator loads nothing.

16
00:00:49.498 --> 00:00:51.333
Next asks for the first match.

17
00:00:51.633 --> 00:00:54.233
Then the for loop resumes exactly where it stopped.

18
00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:57.400
Generators chain into pipelines.

19
00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.267
The file is read lazily.

20
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:01.700
Each stage filters or transforms.

21
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.067
And nothing computes until the final consumer pulls.

22
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:08.233
This is how modern AI apps stream.

23
00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:12.233
A generator yields LLM tokens the instant they arrive.

24
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:18.467
FastAPI's streaming response sends them on, so the browser paints words in real time.

25
00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:24.700
Ingestion works the same way: four gigabytes of reports flow through embedding in constant memory.

26
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:26.722
Let's recap.

27
00:01:26.722 --> 00:01:28.367
Yield makes a generator.

28
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:31.200
Values come on demand, in constant memory.

29
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.333
You can chain generators into lazy pipelines.

30
00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:36.200
Next up: iterators.
